  "query_preview": "..\nGusty winds in the wake of this mornings cold front will continue \ninto the afternoon hours before returning to much calmer conditons \nthis evening and overnight. Otherwise, a mild and unsettled \nweek is expected as a progressive weather pattern continues \nacross the region bringing multiple waves of precipitation \nchances, and increasing snow levels. \n\nThe main concerns this week:\nThe main concerns will be for debris flows/rockslides,\nparticularly in and around burn scars in the Cascades due to\nsnow melt and rain. Chances of 24 hour rain amounts exceeding an\ninch near the Cascade crest are are up to 40-60+% (NBM, even\nhigher in REFS) Tuesday, and includes up to 45% chance in the\nwestern portion of the Little Giant burn scar. Similar\nprobabilities again for the weekend. As discussed in previous\nforecasts, most rivers should behave, however the Stehekin River\nat Stehekin is showing a 10% chance of reaching Minor Flood\nStage (20.5 ft) on Wednesday. These probabilities decrease\nbriefly for Thursday/ Friday and return with a 10% of exceeding\nMinor Flood Stage on Saturday.\n\nWhen to expect precipitation:\nThe next round of precipitation is expected to pick up tomorrow\nevening across the area. Snow levels will be 5000ft + near the \nCanadian border and 7000ft over SE WA into the Central Panhandle\nMountains. Tuesday follows with another round of precipitation \nprimarily across northern WA and N ID Panhandle, with snow \nlevels continuing to increase into the 7000-8000+ ft range. \n\nThere is very good agreement across ensembles of a brief period\nof ridging over the region Wednesday and Thursday while a \ntrough digs south just off the west coast. Most ensemble data \npoints to the region being influenced by troughing / southwest \nflow for the weekend as a low pressure system develops off the \ncoast of BC. There are some inconsistencies across ensembles of \nhow strong and east this system is placed by the weekend. \nHowever, expect precipitation to return for the weekend along \nwith breezy winds.",
